In the checkout run git status and git diff against the default branch, read the new files and run the test suite yourself. Check that a test exists for every acceptance test of the ticket and was written to fail first, that the code does what the ticket and docs/plans/ ask and nothing outside its slice, and that errors and edge cases are handled. Do not edit files. Finish with verdict "approved" when it is correct and the suite passes, otherwise "changes_requested" with specific, actionable comments.

Audit the documents against the finished change for this ticket:

{{.input.task}}

The builder reports: {{.state.builder.summary}}

The ticket text names its feature ("Feature: <slug>"): the plan is in docs/plans/<feature>/ in the checkout; read it. Write your requirement trace to docs/plans/<feature>/tickets/<ticket-id>.md, a new file for this ticket. Never edit TASKS.md or WAVES.md in a ticket run (tickets of one wave run in parallel and would conflict). Edit ARCHITECTURE.md only if the built design differs from it; then say so in your output, and if the change contradicts the approved design report a gap instead. You may update the project's own docs and README so they describe what the code now does (edit only documents, never code). Then trace every requirement of the PRD that this ticket covers to the code and tests that satisfy it, in that file. Finish with verdict "ok" when every covered requirement is met and the design still holds, otherwise "gap" with the reason (which requirement is unmet or which design assumption is wrong). trace lists {requirement, code, tests} for each requirement you traced.

Open the merge request for this ticket with the open_merge_request tool, with the code and the updated documents together:

